id: rust.axum
kind: rust
title: Axum handlers and extractors
aliases: axum, http server, tower service
tags: http, async, api
questions:
  - how do you structure an Axum service?
invariant: A handler is an async function whose arguments are typed extractors, and the router turns it into a Tower service.
answer_30s: Handlers take extractors (path, query, JSON, state) and return IntoResponse, so request validation and response mapping are typed and compose with Tower layers.
answer_2m: |
  The extractor model is the thing to be able to explain: ordering matters
  (body-consuming extractors last), rejection types are how bad input becomes a
  4xx, and State<Arc<AppState>> is how shared resources reach handlers without
  globals. Middleware is a Tower layer rather than handler code, so timeouts,
  tracing, compression and request ids are configuration. The production shape:
  state built once with validation, router composed from nested routers, layers
  in a deliberate order, and shutdown driven by axum::serve with a cancellation
  signal.
when_to_use:
  - HTTP APIs, webhooks, internal services
minimal_example: |
  use axum::{extract::{Path, State}, routing::get, Json, Router};

  async fn get_order(State(st): State<AppState>, Path(id): Path<String>)
      -> Result<Json<Order>, AppError> {
      Ok(Json(st.db.load(&id).await?))
  }

  fn app(st: AppState) -> Router {
      Router::new().route("/orders/:id", get(get_order)).with_state(st)
          .layer(tower_http::trace::TraceLayer::new_for_http())
  }
failure_modes:
  - returning 500 for client errors because the error mapping is missing
  - blocking work (hashing, std::fs) on a runtime worker instead of spawn_blocking
  - no request body limit, so a large upload exhausts memory
related:
  - rust.tower
  - rust.serde
  - rust.blocking-in-async
  - rust.app-state
  - rust.tracing
  - recipe.rest-api
sources:
  - https://docs.rs/axum/latest/axum/
